   "args": {
    "text": "Verdict: not supported\n\nThe sentence’s definition is stated in *Model evaluation for extreme risks* itself: mechanistic analysis is described as studying a model’s weights and activations to understand how it functions. The cited 2020 source, Olah et al.’s *Zoom In: An Introduction to Circuits*, does support the broad idea of understanding neural networks by investigating features and circuits: its outline sets out “Claim 1: Features” and “Claim 2: Circuits,” with sections on feature visualization and circuit examples (distill:zoom-in/r1/b20; distill:zoom-in/r1/b67; distill:zoom-in/r1/b33; distill:zoom-in/r1/b72).\n\nBut I could not find the cited Nanda et al. 2023 work in the library. The available 2023 page that mentions mechanistic interpretability only lists *Progress measures for grokking via mechanistic interpretability* in its references (arxiv:2305.15324/v2/p17); it does not show that paper’s contents. So the citation to Olah et al. broadly aligns with mechanistic analysis, but the cited sources available here do not establish the particular definition that it means studying model weights and activations. The sentence is therefore not supported by the cited-source evidence available in the library."
   },
